          <p style={{ fontSize: 13, color: T.muted, lineHeight: 1.7, marginBottom: 0 }}>
            She didn't invent polar area charts. She sent them to Queen Victoria instead
            of a letter because she knew the Queen wouldn't read a table. The blue wedges
            are preventable diseases, red is death from wounds, dark is other causes. The
            blue wedges swallow everything else — and that <em>is</em> the argument. More
            soldiers died from bad sanitation than from Russian bullets.
          </p>
          <WikiLink
            href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Florence_Nightingale#Statistics_and_sanitary_reform"
            label="Nightingale's statistical graphics"
          />
          <DivePrompt prompt="Create a polar area chart (coxcomb) where each wedge is a month. Wedge angle is equal; area encodes magnitude. Use blue-grey for the dominant category, red for secondary, dark for tertiary. The largest wedges should be visually overwhelming — that is the argument." />
          <KeyLesson text="A chart is an argument. Make the overwhelming wedge overwhelming. If you need a legend, your chart isn't doing its job." />
        </div>
      }
      chart={
        <ChartFrame
          legend={[
            { color: "#8b9fae", label: "Preventable disease" },
            { color: "#c45b6a", label: "Wounds" },
            { color: "#3a3a3a", label: "Other causes" },
          ]}
          footnote="Recreation of Nightingale's 1858 Diagram of the Causes of Mortality. Data is illustrative."
        >
          <NightingaleRose />
        </ChartFrame>
      }
    />
  );

  if (index === 1) return (
    <SplitRow
      text={
        <div>
          <SectionTitle name="W.E.B. Du Bois" subtitle="Data as Testimony, 1900" />
          <p style={{ fontSize: 13, color: T.muted, lineHeight: 1.7, marginBottom: 0 }}>
            Hand-drawn for a white European audience at the Paris Exposition. These charts
            showed family income and expenditure of 150 Black families in Atlanta. As
            income rose, the share spent on rent barely fell while "other" spending grew —
            revealing that even middle-class Black families were trapped in a housing market
            that extracted disproportionate rent. Every asymmetry is a deliberate
            rhetorical choice.
          </p>
          <WikiLink
            href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W._E._B._Du_Bois%27s_hand-drawn_infographics"
            label="Du Bois's data portraits"
          />
          <DivePrompt prompt="Create a horizontal stacked bar chart in the Du Bois 1900 style. Limited palette: black, deep red, gold, brown. Georgia serif font. Uppercase title with wide letter-spacing. No gridlines. The asymmetry between categories tells the story without annotation." />
          <KeyLesson text="Color is rhetoric, not cosmetics. Every visual choice — palette, proportion, asymmetry — carries the argument." />
        </div>
      }
      chart={
        <ChartFrame footnote="Recreation of Du Bois's Income and Expenditure plate, Paris Exposition 1900. Data is illustrative.">
          <p style={{
            fontSize: 10, fontFamily: "Georgia, serif", textAlign: "center",
            color: "#2d2d2d", marginBottom: 12, textTransform: "uppercase", letterSpacing: 2,
          }}>
            Income and Expenditure of 150 Negro Families in Atlanta, GA, U.S.A.
          </p>
          <DuBoisBars />
        </ChartFrame>
      }
    />
  );

  return (
    <SplitRow
      text={
        <div>
          <SectionTitle name="Captain Sankey" subtitle="Where the Energy Goes, 1898" />
          <p style={{ fontSize: 13, color: T.muted, lineHeight: 1.7, marginBottom: 0 }}>
            Before Minard mapped Napoleon's losses, Captain Sankey used width to show
            energy flowing through a steam engine. Of 100 units of coal energy, only 30
            became useful work — the rest bled away as flue gases, radiation,
            condensation, and friction. The tapering tells the whole story: width is
            quantity, and every narrowing is a loss you can feel.
          </p>
          <WikiLink
            href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sankey_diagram"
            label="Sankey diagram"
          />
          <DivePrompt prompt="Create a Sankey diagram where width encodes flow magnitude. Show a total quantity splitting and diminishing through stages. Muted blue for main flow, red for losses, green for useful output. Label each node with its value. The tapering makes the losses viscerally obvious." />
          <KeyLesson text="Width is quantity. When the flow narrows, the reader feels the loss without being told. The best encoding is the one that doesn't need explanation." />
        </div>
      }
      chart={
        <ChartFrame
          legend={[
            { color: "#8b9fae", label: "Energy flow" },
            { color: "#c45b6a", label: "Energy loss" },
            { color: "#2d7a00", label: "Useful work" },
          ]}
          footnote="Recreation of Captain Sankey's 1898 steam engine energy diagram. Data is illustrative."
        >
          <SankeyDiagram />
        </ChartFrame>
      }
    />
  );
}

