                <div style={{ display: "flex", alignItems: "center", gap: 4 }}>
                  <div style={{ width: 10, height: 10, background: "#fff", borderRadius: 1 }} />
                  <span style={{ fontSize: 10, color: "#999" }}>Non-reporting country</span>
                </div>
                <div style={{ display: "flex", alignItems: "center", gap: 4 }}>
                  <div style={{ width: 10, height: 10, background: "#FFDE02", borderRadius: 1 }} />
                  <span style={{ fontSize: 10, color: "#999" }}>Country with reporting</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <p style={{ fontSize: 9, color: "#666", marginTop: 6, fontStyle: "italic" }}>Isotype-style dot array using WHO air quality data. Each dot = one country.</p>
            </div>
          )}
        </div>
      }
    />
  );

  if (index === 1) return (
    <SplitRow
      text={
        <div>
          <SectionTitle name="Gestalt Principles" subtitle="Designing for the Pre-attentive Pass" />
          <p style={{ fontSize: 13, color: T.muted, lineHeight: 1.7, marginBottom: 0 }}>
            Before a viewer <em>thinks</em> about your chart, their visual cortex has already
            processed it. Gestalt psychology identified the rules: we group by proximity,
            by similarity, by enclosure. We complete shapes that aren't closed. We separate
            figure from ground instantly. These aren't aesthetic preferences — they're
            neurological constraints. A chart that fights them fails before it's read.
          </p>
          <WikiLink href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gestalt_psychology#Pr%C3%A4gnanz" label="Gestalt principles of perception" />
          <DivePrompt prompt="Show the same bar chart twice: first with random colors on every bar, then with one bar highlighted in color and everything else in grey. The second version uses figure-ground and similarity to make the signal pop pre-attentively. No annotation needed." />
          <KeyLesson text="Your reader's visual cortex processes the chart before their conscious mind does. Design for that first pass — proximity groups, similarity connects, figure-ground separates signal from noise." />
        </div>
      }
      chart={
        <div style={{ display: "flex", flexDirection: "column", gap: 16 }}>
          <GestaltBars />
          <GestaltDots />
        </div>
      }
    />
  );

  if (index === 2) return (
    <SplitRow
      text={
        <div>
          <SectionTitle name="The Grid System" subtitle="Müller-Brockmann & Modular Order, 1961" />
          <p style={{ fontSize: 13, color: T.muted, lineHeight: 1.7, marginBottom: 0 }}>
            Josef Müller-Brockmann's grid wasn't a cage — it was a contract between designer
            and reader. Every element snaps to the same invisible scaffold. Columns align with
            columns. Baselines align with baselines. The reader doesn't see the grid,
            but they feel the order. When something breaks the grid, it means something.
            When nothing breaks it, the data speaks without competition.
          </p>
          <WikiLink href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grid_(graphic_design)" label="Grid systems in graphic design" />
          <DivePrompt prompt="Create a dashboard where every element aligns to a strict grid. KPIs in equal columns, charts baseline-aligned. No overlapping, no arbitrary offsets. The grid is invisible but the reader feels the structure. Use consistent spacing units (multiples of 8px)." />
          <KeyLesson text="The grid is a contract with the reader. When every element snaps to the same scaffold, the data speaks without competition. Break the grid only when you mean it." />
        </div>
      }
      chart={
        <ChartFrame bg="#fff" footnote="A Müller-Brockmann-style modular grid. Every element aligns to the same invisible structure.">
          <GridDemo />
        </ChartFrame>
      }
    />
  );

  if (index === 3) return (
    <SplitRow
      text={
        <div>
          <SectionTitle name="Helvetica & Type Hierarchy" subtitle="Max Miedinger, 1957" />
          <p style={{ fontSize: 13, color: T.muted, lineHeight: 1.7, marginBottom: 0 }}>
            Helvetica was designed to disappear. No serifs to distract, no quirks to remember.
            Swiss typographers proved that hierarchy comes from contrast: size against size,
            weight against weight, black against grey. You don't need ornament, color, or
            decoration. Two weights of the same font and careful spacing are enough to
            organize any amount of information.
          </p>
          <WikiLink href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helvetica" label="Helvetica typeface" />
          <DivePrompt prompt="Style a data summary using only two font weights: bold for labels and metrics, regular for context. No colors except black and grey. Use font-size contrast (large numbers, small labels) and whitespace instead of borders or backgrounds to create hierarchy." />
          <KeyLesson text="Two weights, two sizes, two shades. That's enough to organize any chart. If you need color to distinguish a label from a value, your typography isn't working." />
        </div>
      }
      chart={
        <div>
          <TypographyDemo />
          <ChartFootnote text="Inspired by Massimo Vignelli's typographic posters. Structure through contrast alone." />
        </div>
      }
    />
  );

  return (
    <SplitRow
      text={
        <div>
          <SectionTitle name="The Silence Between" subtitle="Whitespace as Information" />
          <p style={{ fontSize: 13, color: T.muted, lineHeight: 1.7, marginBottom: 0 }}>
            Jan Tschichold called empty space "the lungs of a design." Swiss designers treated
            whitespace not as absence but as a structural element — it groups, it separates,
            it breathes. In a chart, the space between bars tells you they're different
            categories. The margin around a number tells you it matters. Remove a border and
            replace it with space: the information is the same, but the noise is gone.
          </p>
          <WikiLink href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_space_(visual_arts)" label="Whitespace in visual design" />
          <DivePrompt prompt="Create two versions of the same bar chart: one with gridlines, borders, backgrounds, and color fills; another with only the bars, direct labels, and whitespace. The second version should feel lighter and communicate faster." />
          <KeyLesson text="Whitespace is not empty. It groups, it separates, it breathes. Every border you remove is a signal you're no longer asking the reader to process." />
        </div>
      }
      chart={
        <ChartFrame bg="#fff" footnote="Before and after: the same data with and without visual noise. Whitespace replaces borders.">
          <WhitespaceDemo />
        </ChartFrame>
      }
    />
  );
}

/* ── Tab 2 chart components ─────────────────────────────────────── */

function GestaltBars() {
  const svgRef = useRef<SVGSVGElement>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!svgRef.current) return;
    const svg = d3.select(svgRef.current);
    svg.selectAll("*").remove();

    const w = 520, h = 180;
    svg.attr("viewBox", `0 0 ${w} ${h}`);

    const data = [38, 22, 45, 15, 31, 28, 42];
    const labels = ["Mon", "Tue", "Wed", "Thu", "Fri", "Sat", "Sun"];
    const barW = 24, gap = 6;
    const chartW = data.length * (barW + gap) - gap;
    const maxVal = 50;
    const barAreaH = 110;
    const barBase = 145;
    const highlightIdx = 2;

    const badColors = ["#e74c3c", "#3498db", "#2ecc71", "#f39c12", "#9b59b6", "#1abc9c", "#e67e22"];
    const halfW = w / 2 - 16;
    const leftStart = (halfW - chartW) / 2;
    const rightStart = w / 2 + 16 + (halfW - chartW) / 2;

    svg.append("text").attr("x", halfW / 2).attr("y", 18)
      .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 10).attr("font-weight", 700)
      .attr("fill", T.fg).attr("font-family", T.sans).text("Similarity: every bar screams");

    svg.append("text").attr("x", w / 2 + 16 + halfW / 2).attr("y", 18)
      .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 10).attr("font-weight", 700)
      .attr("fill", T.fg).attr("font-family", T.sans).text("Figure-ground: one bar speaks");

    svg.append("line").attr("x1", w / 2).attr("y1", 8).attr("x2", w / 2).attr("y2", h - 4)
      .attr("stroke", T.rule).attr("stroke-width", 1).attr("stroke-dasharray", "3,3");

    data.forEach((d, i) => {
      const barH = (d / maxVal) * barAreaH;
      const lx = leftStart + i * (barW + gap);
      svg.append("rect").attr("x", lx).attr("y", barBase - barH).attr("width", barW).attr("height", barH)
        .attr("fill", badColors[i]);
      svg.append("text").attr("x", lx + barW / 2).attr("y", barBase + 14)
        .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 8).attr("fill", T.muted)
        .attr("font-family", T.sans).text(labels[i]);

      const rx = rightStart + i * (barW + gap);
      svg.append("rect").attr("x", rx).attr("y", barBase - barH).attr("width", barW).attr("height", barH)
        .attr("fill", i === highlightIdx ? T.fg : "#d9d9d9");
      svg.append("text").attr("x", rx + barW / 2).attr("y", barBase + 14)
        .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 8)
        .attr("fill", i === highlightIdx ? T.fg : T.muted)
        .attr("font-weight", i === highlightIdx ? 700 : 400)
        .attr("font-family", T.sans).text(labels[i]);
    });

    const hx = rightStart + highlightIdx * (barW + gap);
    const hBarH = (data[highlightIdx] / maxVal) * barAreaH;
    svg.append("text").attr("x", hx + barW / 2).attr("y", barBase - hBarH - 6)
      .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 11).attr("font-weight", 700)
      .attr("fill", T.fg).attr("font-family", T.sans).text(data[highlightIdx]);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div style={{ background: "#fff", padding: "16px 16px 10px" }}>
      <svg ref={svgRef} style={{ width: "100%", height: "auto", aspectRatio: "520/180" }} />
      <ChartFootnote text="Similarity & figure-ground: same data, different perceptual results." />
    </div>
  );
}

function GestaltDots() {
  const svgRef = useRef<SVGSVGElement>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!svgRef.current) return;
    const svg = d3.select(svgRef.current);
    svg.selectAll("*").remove();

    const w = 520, h = 100;
    svg.attr("viewBox", `0 0 ${w} ${h}`);

    const dotR = 5, dotGap = 14, groupGap = 32;
    const halfW = w / 2 - 16;

    svg.append("text").attr("x", halfW / 2).attr("y", 16)
      .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 10).attr("font-weight", 700)
      .attr("fill", T.fg).attr("font-family", T.sans).text("Proximity: twelve equal dots");

    svg.append("text").attr("x", w / 2 + 16 + halfW / 2).attr("y", 16)
      .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 10).attr("font-weight", 700)
      .attr("fill", T.fg).attr("font-family", T.sans).text("Proximity: three groups of four");

    svg.append("line").attr("x1", w / 2).attr("y1", 6).attr("x2", w / 2).attr("y2", h - 4)
      .attr("stroke", T.rule).attr("stroke-width", 1).attr("stroke-dasharray", "3,3");

    // Even spacing
    const evenTotalW = 11 * dotGap;
    const evenStart = (halfW - evenTotalW) / 2;
    for (let i = 0; i < 12; i++) {
      svg.append("circle")
        .attr("cx", evenStart + i * dotGap).attr("cy", 44).attr("r", dotR)
        .attr("fill", T.fg);
    }

    // Grouped
    const groupInner = 3 * dotGap;
    const groupedTotalW = 3 * groupInner + 2 * groupGap;
    const groupStart = w / 2 + 16 + (halfW - groupedTotalW) / 2;
    for (let gi = 0; gi < 3; gi++) {
      for (let ci = 0; ci < 4; ci++) {
        svg.append("circle")
          .attr("cx", groupStart + gi * (groupInner + groupGap) + ci * dotGap)
          .attr("cy", 44).attr("r", dotR)
          .attr("fill", T.fg);
      }
      // Bracket
      const gx = groupStart + gi * (groupInner + groupGap);
      const gxEnd = gx + 3 * dotGap;
      svg.append("line").attr("x1", gx).attr("y1", 58).attr("x2", gxEnd).attr("y2", 58)
        .attr("stroke", T.muted).attr("stroke-width", 0.8);
      svg.append("text").attr("x", (gx + gxEnd) / 2).attr("y", 70)
        .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 8).attr("fill", T.muted)
        .attr("font-family", T.sans).text(`Group ${gi + 1}`);
    }

    svg.append("text").attr("x", w / 2).attr("y", h - 4)
      .attr("text-anchor", "middle").attr("font-size", 9).attr("fill", T.muted)
      .attr("font-family", T.sans).attr("font-style", "italic")
      .text("Same 12 dots. Spacing alone creates structure.");
  }, []);

  return (
    <div style={{ background: "#fff", padding: "16px 16px 10px" }}>
      <svg ref={svgRef} style={{ width: "100%", height: "auto", aspectRatio: "520/100" }} />
    </div>
  );
}

